-- pitfalls --

only plain objects (non-null, non-array) get merged into data. if a step
returns a string, number, array, null, or undefined, the value is stored
in stepState.output but silently not merged. there is no warning. access
non-object outputs via getStep(name).output instead.

in cyclic workflows, do not hardcode `<executionId>:<stepName>` as the
dedup key for external side effects - read step.idempotencyKey from the
context. the key gains a `:<pass>` suffix from the second pass onward,
and hardcoding the two-part form suppresses side effects on every pass
after the first.

a step interrupted mid-flight (pause, cancel, lease loss) has already
consumed a retry attempt - attempts increments when the step starts, not
when it completes. after resume the re-run consumes another. with
maxAttempts: 1, a mid-step pause/resume leaves no retry budget if the
re-run fails.

-- gotchas --

engine.ready() is called implicitly by start/cancel/resume/runDue/etc,
but storage.init() can be slow (creates tables). the first call that
triggers ready() pays this cost. call engine.ready() explicitly at
startup if you need predictable latency.

the step context clones input, data, metadata, steps, and params -
mutations inside a step function do not leak to the next step. but the
clone uses JSON.parse(JSON.stringify()), so Date objects become strings,
undefined values are dropped, and anything not JSON-serializable is lost.

pausing does not stop the clock on wait timeouts. the deadline is
restored as-is on resume, so a deadline that passed during the pause
fires the timeout route immediately.

signal() on a paused execution throws AlreadySignaledError - the same
error used for duplicate signals - even though nothing signaled it.
resume first, then signal.

exceeding maxPasses fails the step that routes into the looped step, not
the looped step itself. the error message names the looped step, but
retry rules and journal attribution belong to the routing step.

listExecutions supports limit but no cursor or offset. pagination
through large result sets is not currently possible.

journal is unbounded by default (maxJournalEntries: 0), and every save
serializes the full journal. long-lived executions with many retries or
loop passes pay growing save costs - set maxJournalEntries to cap it.

-- integration --

postgresDriver requires explicit connection config - either
connectionString or individual host/port/database/user/password params.
there are no built-in defaults. the pg library's own env var fallbacks
(PGHOST, PGPORT, etc.) apply if individual params are omitted.

sqliteDriver requires a filename. there is no in-memory sqlite mode
exposed - use memoryDriver for ephemeral storage.

storage drivers are plain objects with async methods, not classes.
to write a custom driver, implement: init, createExecution, getExecution,
saveExecution (CAS on both expectedLockOwner and expectedStatus),
claimAvailable (must be atomic/transactional and must exclude the paused
status), renewLease, listExecutions, close. findChildren is optional but
without it cascade cancellation of subworkflow children silently does
nothing.

-- anti-patterns-and-pitfalls --

claimAvailable only reclaims executions with status queued/waiting/suspended. An execution that was RUNNING a step when its worker process died keeps status 'running' and is never picked up again after its lease expires - the run is stranded forever. Apps must recover these themselves (e.g. at boot: find running executions with expired leases via the storage driver, reset status to 'queued', clear the lock, set availableAt=now; the step re-runs, so handlers must be idempotent). Long-running activity steps (minutes-long model calls) hit this on every process restart.
